==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈbɪkə/}}
*: {{rhymes|ɪkə(r)}}

===Etymology===
{{etyl|enm}} {{term|bikeren|lang=enm}} ‘to attack’, from {{etyl|dum}} {{term|bicken|lang=nl}} ‘to stab, attack’ (modern {{term|bikken|lang=nl}} ‘to hack’), from {{proto|Germanic|bikjanan|lang=en}} (compare Old English {{term|becca|lang=ang}} ‘pickax’, German {{term|picken|lang=de}} ‘to peck, pick at’, Old Norse {{term|bikkja|lang=non}} ‘to plunge into water’), from {{proto|Indo-European|bʰeg-|lang=en}} ‘to smash, break’.

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[quarrel]] in a [[tiresome]], insulting manner.
#: ''They '''bickered''' about dinner every evening.''
# To move [[tremulously]], quiver, shimmer (of a water stream, of a flame)
#*XIX cent, ''[[s:The Brook (Tennyson)|The Brook]]'', by [[w:Alfred, Lord Tennyson|Tennyson]]
#*: I come from haunts of coot and hern, \ I make a sudden sally, \ And sparkle out among the fern, \ To '''bicker''' down a valley.

====Synonyms====
* [[wrangle]]
* See also [[Wikisaurus:squabble]]

